{"text": "JPMorgan’s market risk does not look worse in the current desk read than a year ago. Its 30-day volatility is 14.2% [f_169a50b18a33], its beta to SPY is 0.78× [f_6652f20cb246], and it is -3.18% from its 52-week high [f_19045dbaf56a]. I do not have prior comparable readings for the same three measures on a twelve-month-ago basis, so I cannot prove a change from that date; I can only say the current snapshot is fairly restrained for a bank with a beta below 1. The 1-year price return is not needed to answer that comparison, and I am leaving it out.

On the income side, interest expense is $5.55B [f_d2ec527d5e6c] in 2021-12-31, $26.10B [f_d2ec527d5e6c] in 2022-12-31, and $81.32B [f_d2ec527d5e6c] in 2023-12-31. The pretax-to-net-income gap is $12.06B [f_dce448c3517f] in 2023-12-31, $16.61B [f_dce448c3517f] in 2024-12-31, and $15.55B [f_dce448c3517f] in 2025-12-31. That gap is the desk’s derived figure, and it has stayed in the low-to-mid teens of billions, which does not point to a sudden deterioration in the spread between pretax and bottom-line earnings.

For a bank, the usual issuer methods that do not apply are the ones the desk flags as not for a financial issuer: ebit, ebitda, free_cash_flow, total_debt, net_debt, ebit_interest_coverage, debt_to_ebitda, debt_to_operating_cash_flow, fcf_to_debt, current_ratio, quick_ratio, gross_margin, operating_margin, net_margin, days_sales_outstanding, days_inventory, days_payable, nopat, invested_capital, roic, asset_turnover, quick_assets, fcf_margin, capex_intensity, net_debt_to_ebitda, and cash_conversion_cycle. The practical implication is that bank risk has to be read through balance-sheet, funding, and market-sensitivity lenses instead of industrial-style operating ratios."}

